  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_df8acf8229304913b535d1efe2729734",
      "statement": "An optimization problem in standard form minimizes f_0(x) over x∈R^n subject to inequality constraints f_i(x)≤0 and equality constraints h_i(x)=0, with the optimal value p* the infimum of f_0 over the feasible set, taking p*=+∞ when infeasible (infimum of the empty set) and p*=−∞ when unbounded below.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L16-L32",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:3ff41c0ce553fd8f4170090c0aa71835665790799f6e3b1be55449ad4bc061eb:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_28aba09ee77c4c39b6b67d81f5bbf7e0",
      "statement": "A point is locally optimal if it minimizes f_0 among feasible points within some radius R, and the zero-objective special case is the feasibility problem — deciding whether the constraints are consistent and exhibiting a satisfying point — while maximization is handled by minimizing −f_0.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L34-L44",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:393e73e9aba377f7da0cb490222f364367a999645cae06100523dde0aa349443:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ee875fbe35da43d1aad41b0f04ceac86",
      "statement": "A convex optimization problem requires a convex objective, convex inequality-constraint functions, and affine equality constraints a_i'x=b_i, from which the feasible set is convex; if the objective is strictly convex the optimal set contains at most one point.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L48-L58",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:12a3526a55885bf701ea66e6a8a27946ebf2dc4fb5d990d472b858355baba70b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e2fbce7c58884f59ba5a216ea6996b64",
      "statement": "In a convex problem every locally optimal point is globally optimal, proved by taking a convex combination z=(1−θ)x+θy with θ=R/(2‖y−x‖) of a hypothetical better feasible point y with the local optimum x, which is feasible, within R/2<R, and by convexity strictly better — contradicting local optimality.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L60-L72",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:f44a191c6a7327a7d12df779a9e0ce5b6af9782ee9711667e500427996dd3abd:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_01146e4b6c0b4de0b20be7104cd431ca",
      "statement": "For a differentiable convex objective, x in the feasible set X is optimal if and only if ∇f_0(x)'(y−x)≥0 for every y∈X, reducing in the unconstrained case to ∇f_0(x)=0.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L74-L78",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:bec5d51551da3b6bf75d1d1b059bdfae013f327d941ebcaba2f6b0f5da21975c:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_b8ba004a74dc4508be4d94022c6860cf",
      "statement": "The tractable convex classes nest by increasing generality: LP (affine objective and constraints, polyhedral feasible set), QP (convex quadratic objective with P∈S^n_+ over a polyhedron, LP being P=0), and QCQP (convex quadratic inequality constraints too, an intersection of ellipsoids when P_i≻0), with QCQP including QP and hence LP.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L84-L100",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5fa26d04a0c1aa05cc39ad54cf6e3e92b6d7178f6cd36dc10a7be99b56ec4364:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_d99f563956bb4439a0fced69102bb355",
      "statement": "An SOCP minimizes f'x subject to second-order cone constraints ‖A_i x+b_i‖₂≤c_i'x+d_i, equivalent to requiring the affine map (A_i x+b_i, c_i'x+d_i) to lie in the second-order (Lorentz/ice-cream) cone C_k={(u,t):‖u‖₂≤t}, making the feasible set the convex inverse image of a cone under an affine map.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L102-L114",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fe782d3354077a2a41815bf3f225576a2f59fc390c4d3ec35cc126eb1629cb1b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_782ea86bf90a497896491d4f4f846ca4",
      "statement": "Setting A_i=0 reduces an SOCP to an LP and c_i=0 reduces it to a QCQP (via squaring ‖A_i x+b_i‖≤d_i), a QCQP with P_i≻0 converts to SOCP by completing the square x'P_i x+2q_i'x+r_i=‖P_i^{1/2}x+P_i^{-1/2}q_i‖₂²+r_i−q_i'P_i^{-1}q_i, and because each class is a special case of the next an SOCP algorithm automatically solves QCQP, QP and LP.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L116-L124",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:afe95fbcf5e91e81f46dba69518b8fcf9fa3cdf131f34392de042c22079fdd14:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3b4cd2105aba415c9b795b0e3d5d46ee",
      "statement": "Markowitz's problem minimizes x'Σx subject to p̄'x≥r_min, 1'x=1, x⪰0 and is a QP because the objective is convex quadratic (Σ⪰0) and the constraints are affine.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L128-L132",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:81dbcc1d1414728d1f7d4c522206ee8364f78084442da1267aa6edb6af3402de:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_9354a59d90a74668962fd2543c9ea97e",
      "statement": "Extensions staying within QP include short positions via x=x_long−x_short with a cap 1'x_short≤η·1'x_long limiting total short to a fraction η of long, and linear transaction costs with buy/sell variables and a self-financing constraint keeping the problem a QP.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L134-L134",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:080caeeaa4c0bbc3bd34e63ed7d958bc74877056cb21d045b61eb9254ca29e6f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_d320317d65194b1fa9d3fe40f007b5e3",
      "statement": "Under Gaussian price changes a loss-risk constraint prob(r≤α)≤β becomes p̄'x+Φ⁻¹(β)‖Σ^{1/2}x‖₂≥α, which is a second-order cone constraint whenever β≤1/2 (so Φ⁻¹(β)≤0), making the maximize-return-under-shortfall problem an SOCP.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L136-L148",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a93de81a858ebc9c9afda46898479daf581dd1dd933a77da339916bf7932a927:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_d4f39358a7a246f09da369165cba5945",
      "statement": "In robust optimization, if each a_i lies in an ellipsoid E_i={ā_i+P_i u:‖u‖₂≤1}, the robust constraint sup{a_i'x}=ā_i'x+‖P_i'x‖₂≤b_i is second-order conic and the norm term acts as regularization, discouraging x from large values in directions of high uncertainty.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L148-L152",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:2f454991f5d5983979d0fd1adab455a11843718da95917f262615a2ba13b421d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_3546d4ae3baa4cc1ab095d6d1569e4ce",
      "statement": "The Lagrange dual function g(λ,ν)=inf_x L(x,λ,ν) is the pointwise infimum of affine functions of (λ,ν), hence concave even when the primal is not convex, and lower-bounds the optimal value g(λ,ν)≤p* for every λ⪰0 and every ν.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L156-L176",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fa096320461fbf340646bbb3ee930a8c46b9b85c9841002abc38c9941dd06411:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_bf0ce2e2ba2240b78e676ee317808896",
      "statement": "The Lagrange dual problem maximizes g(λ,ν) subject to λ⪰0 and is always convex regardless of the primal; weak duality d*≤p* always holds (even nonconvex or infinite), with p*−d* the optimal duality gap.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L178-L188",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ef29d417a76b17cf380b42ba6106761bd892c1c69d382cd208f8eaaaf439cdc8:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_5a73430e60e54b9baff8ad97b610fd30",
      "statement": "Strong duality d*=p* does not hold in general but usually holds for convex problems, and Slater's condition — a strictly feasible point x∈relint D with f_i(x)<0 and Ax=b — guarantees strong duality for a convex problem, can be relaxed so affine inequalities need not be strict, and ensures the dual optimum is attained when d*>−∞.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L190-L202",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:afc03fd15f71ba6946a7977123c6b3587250749a37cbbfdb4a02551721695c4d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ce87240de16e4d4e9543368261ae72b1",
      "statement": "A feasible dual point certifies suboptimality via the duality gap f_0(x)−g(λ,ν), localizing p*∈[g(λ,ν), f_0(x)], which underpins non-heuristic stopping criteria for optimization algorithms.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L204-L204",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:9b2da663241dc3fbb861f7f8146872dd93ae73a40dc57c3f6768619d41afbe8d:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_5af7825aa0be4476bf80a625f1b546d0",
      "statement": "When strong duality holds, every primal-dual optimal pair satisfies complementary slackness λ_i* f_i(x*)=0 for all i, so each optimal multiplier is zero unless its constraint is active at the optimum.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L208-L218",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a6a42946d0cad6d64fe6f1e2c491a14f5b38d303b7eb5dc8abb02f24fed4db7c:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_bce975d837f040809eed9604f61cc354",
      "statement": "The KKT conditions — primal feasibility, dual feasibility λ_i*≥0, complementary slackness, and stationarity ∇f_0(x*)+Σλ_i*∇f_i(x*)+Σν_i*∇h_i(x*)=0 — must be satisfied by every primal-dual optimal pair of any differentiable problem for which strong duality holds; they are due to Kuhn and Tucker (1951) and earlier to Karush's 1939 thesis.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L220-L224",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:ef2db39bf7900f8d4e672e6cdb5440cd064b8b4aa2d370517718b4b5c085f0da:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_8021ce01e46d44a2a4191c9a74b3b2c4",
      "statement": "When the primal is convex (f_i convex, h_i affine) the KKT conditions are also sufficient: any (x̃,λ̃,ν̃) satisfying them are primal and dual optimal with zero duality gap, because λ̃_i≥0 makes the Lagrangian convex, stationarity makes x̃ its minimizer, and complementary slackness collapses g(λ̃,ν̃) to f_0(x̃); under Slater's condition KKT becomes necessary and sufficient.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L226-L234",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:926a5b729c595d729e1603bf950f73d708428263abbec2974c9ce4dadd5f72cd:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_c83128b9afc74660aad148c5dcf749fe",
      "statement": "Equality-constrained convex quadratic minimization has KKT conditions Ax*=b and Px*+q+A'ν*=0, i.e. the linear system [[P, A'],[A, 0]][x*;ν*]=[−q; b] whose solution gives the optimal primal and dual variables, though in most cases KKT has no analytic solution and many convex algorithms are methods for solving it.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L236-L244",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:f1ce9df914d8446fe00e809d5a9eea38e70c823a470286f061b3b1185f0d9f30:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_d575600863844a739bcc7fdc9d5975ef",
      "statement": "SOCP has its own duality theory — its dual is again an SOCP, weak duality follows from a nonnegative Cauchy-Schwarz gap, and strong duality holds if primal or dual is strictly feasible — and interior-point methods use the second-order cone barrier φ(u,t)=−log(t²−u'u) in a primal-dual potential-reduction method specializing Nesterov-Nemirovskii.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L246-L254",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:6ab432a2d715b775b2300ea6300bc0d2592ef51e6d508b479eb452af6e1df21b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a5a64bf7d55f431586c3ca13dae126f6",
      "statement": "For interior-point SOCP methods, worst-case iteration count grows at most as the square root of problem size while in practice the typical count is between 5 and 50, almost independent of problem size, which is why optimization libraries return the global solution of LP, QP, QCQP and SOCP portfolio problems.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/14_convex_optimization_cones_duality_and_kkt.md#L254-L254",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:949c04d6f3e865f9fe185bf46efb25f297544d93a697ff76312f19c5400e2804:"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
